        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Olmecs</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The tropical lowlands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, with Olmec-style art found from central Mexico and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and El Salvador</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1200–400 BCE, with roots going back to c. 1600 BCE or earlier; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a> was the leading centre until c. 900 BCE, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a> until c. 400 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Seventeen colossal stone heads; basalt thrones and other monuments; carved jade and serpentine; the earthen pyramid and buried offerings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>; the oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> balls</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations since the 1930s at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a> and El Manatí; their sculpture, offerings and figurines; no deciphered writing; the name itself comes from Aztec accounts of a later people of the Gulf Coast</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="empire-meta">9 min read · Last reviewed October 2026</p>
      </div>
