    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Olmecs built the first great centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>, in the tropical lowlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>’s Gulf Coast between about 1200 and 400 BCE.</li><li>Their sculptors moved basalt blocks of many tonnes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains">Tuxtla Mountains</a> to carve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a> and thrones, probably portraits and seats of rulers, and worked jade with great skill.</li><li>Their images of rulers, gods and the cosmos spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> and helped shape the art and religion of later peoples such as the Maya, though how far they led that shared culture is debated.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Olmecs’ own name is unknown. “Olmec” comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, in which the Olmeca were the “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> people”, a name for those who lived on the Gulf Coast some two thousand years later.</p></aside>
    </section>

