    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1600 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Offerings, among them the oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> balls, are laid in the sacred spring of El Manatí</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a>, above the Coatzacoalcos river, becomes the largest centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 900 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a> declines, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a> becomes the leading Olmec centre</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 900–500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Olmec-style reliefs are carved on the hillside of Chalcatzingo in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 400 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a> is abandoned, and the population of the Olmec heartland falls sharply</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">32 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Stela C at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a>, an Epi-Olmec monument, records one of the earliest Long Count dates</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1862</span><span class="rf-text">The first known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal head</a> comes to light at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a>, described by José María Melgar y Serrano</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1938–1946</span><span class="rf-text">Matthew Stirling’s expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a> begin Olmec archaeology</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan.jpg" alt="San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CabezaColosal1_MuseoXalapa_v1.1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Madman2001, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Olmecs take their name from a people described in Aztec and early colonial accounts: in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, the language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, the Olmeca were the “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> people” of the Gulf Coast, where rubber trees grew. In the early 20th century scholars applied the name to the ruins of the region and to a distinctive style of carving found there, before it was understood that these were some two thousand years older than the Aztecs’ Olmeca. The name has stuck. What the ancient people called themselves is unknown, and so is their language, though in 1976 the linguists Lyle Campbell and Terrence Kaufman argued from early loanwords in other Mesoamerican languages that it was probably an ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixe">Mixe</a>–Zoquean languages, which are still spoken in and around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-tehuantepec">Isthmus of Tehuantepec</a>.</p><p>The Olmec heartland lies in the hot, wet lowlands along the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>. It is a land of great rivers, such as the Coatzacoalcos and the Papaloapan, of swamps, forest and seasonal floods, overlooked by the volcanic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains">Tuxtla Mountains</a>. The floods renewed the soils of the river levees, and the rivers, lagoons and coast teemed with fish, turtles and shellfish.</p><p>The civilisation grew out of local farming villages of the 2nd millennium BCE. Its earliest known sacred place is El Manatí, a spring and bog at the foot of a hill some 15 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a>, where people laid offerings from about 1600 BCE or earlier. San Lorenzo itself, on a plateau that rises some 50 metres above the floodplain of the Coatzacoalcos, grew into a great centre during the San Lorenzo phase, which archaeologists now date to about 1400–1000 BCE, and from about 1200 BCE it was the largest settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. The traditional span of the Olmec civilisation, about 1200–400 BCE, therefore marks its flowering rather than its earliest roots. Its distinctive art, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a>, thrones and images of supernatural beings, appeared at San Lorenzo in this period and seems to have developed there from local traditions rather than arriving from elsewhere.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/la-venta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-venta.jpg" alt="La Venta" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Venta_Pir%C3%A1mide_cara_norte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alfonsobouchot, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a> the Olmecs reshaped a whole landscape. The core of the plateau, some 55 hectares, was raised and levelled with hundreds of thousands, perhaps millions, of cubic metres of earth carried in baskets, and its terraces were held by retaining walls. Leading families lived in large houses set on low clay platforms; one building, which archaeologists call the Red Palace, had roof supports of basalt columns. Lines of U-shaped stone drains, covered with lids, carried water to the edges of the plateau. One estimate suggests that the centre housed some 5,500 people and its surrounding district perhaps 13,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>, which succeeded it, stood on a low island in the coastal swamps of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>. At its centre, aligned slightly west of north, rose an earthen pyramid 34 metres high, built of some 100,000 cubic metres of earth and originally stepped, with platforms, courts and plazas laid out to the north; a centre of this size needed the food and labour of a wide region, perhaps 18,000 people or more.</p><p>Farmers grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, which became more important over the centuries, together with beans and squash, and they fished, hunted deer and raised dogs for food. Traces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> on a pot from El Manatí of about 1750 BCE are among the earliest evidence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> for the use of the plant from which chocolate is made. Craft workers carved basalt, jade and serpentine, polished mirrors of iron ore and made fine pottery and figurines, while farming hamlets spread along the rivers.</p><p>No text names an Olmec ruler, but the monuments suggest that power lay with hereditary lords who were also religious leaders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a>, each with its own features and headdress, are usually seen as portraits of individual rulers, and the great rectangular stones once called altars are now understood as thrones. Rulers probably drew their authority from ritual, from their control of land along the fertile levees and from long-distance exchange in prized materials such as jade and obsidian. Whether Olmec society should be called a chiefdom or an early state is still debated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Olmecs stand at the beginning of Mesoamerican religion, the tradition that the peoples of the region shared and transformed for some three thousand years. Since they left no readable texts, their beliefs are reconstructed from their images, often by comparing them with the gods and myths of later peoples such as the Maya and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, a method that can mislead across so many centuries. Olmec art is full of supernatural beings that combine human and animal features. The most common is a plump, infant-like face with a snarling, toothless mouth and a cleft head, which archaeologists call the were-jaguar, although the jaguar link is debated; others combine the features of caimans, jaguars, birds and serpents. Scholars such as Peter David Joralemon and Karl Taube have tried to identify distinct gods among them, including an early form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> god with maize sprouting from a cleft in his head. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>’s Monument 19, a man framed by the coils of a great crested rattlesnake, is often called the earliest known image of the feathered serpent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>.</p><p>Sacred places in the landscape mattered. At El Manatí people placed jadeite axes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> balls and, later, carved wooden busts, the oldest wooden sculptures found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in the waters of a spring; the bones of infants found there suggest that children were sacrificed, though how they died is not known. Mountains and caves were probably seen as places where the world of the living met the powers of earth and rain. On La Venta’s Altar 4 a ruler sits in a niche shaped like the mouth of a cave or of an earth creature, holding a rope that runs to figures carved on the sides, read either as captives or as ancestors; on Altar 5 a figure holds a limp were-jaguar infant. At Chalcatzingo in central Mexico, a relief known as El Rey shows a figure seated inside a cave beneath rain clouds.</p><p>At La Venta people also buried great offerings that were never meant to be seen again. One held some 50 tonnes of finished serpentine blocks under about 4,000 tonnes of clay, and three mosaic pavements of serpentine, each about 4.5 by 6 metres, were laid out in a design that may represent a mask or a diagram of the cosmos, and then covered over.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads.jpg" alt="Olmec colossal heads" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">Olmec colossal heads</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olmeca_head_in_Villahermosa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Glysiak, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Olmec sculptors worked hard stone with stone tools and abrasives. Seventeen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a> are known: ten from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a>, four from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>, two from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a> and one from La Cobata. The largest stands about 3.4 metres high, the biggest weigh several tens of tonnes, and most were carved from basalt of Cerro Cintepec, a volcano in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains">Tuxtla Mountains</a>, which had to be moved across many kilometres, probably partly on rafts along the rivers. Each head has its own face and headdress, and at least two from San Lorenzo were recarved from older thrones. San Lorenzo has yielded up to 124 sculptures, including thrones, seated figures and animals; many were later broken, defaced or reworked.</p><p>Jade was the most precious material. The finest, a translucent blue-green jadeite, came from the Motagua valley of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, and Olmec carvers shaped it and serpentine into masks, figurines, ear ornaments and ceremonial axes, some incised with the faces of gods. Potters made hollow, white-slipped figures of seated infants and vessels carved with sacred motifs, and craft workers ground and polished concave mirrors of iron ore, which figurines show worn on the chest.</p><p>The Olmecs left no deciphered writing. The Cascajal Block, a slab of serpentine found by road builders in the San Lorenzo region in the late 1990s and published in 2006, bears 62 signs, 28 of them different, arranged in rows. Dated by the pottery found with it to about 1100–900 BCE, it would be the oldest writing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> if it is genuine and correctly dated, but because it was not found in a recorded excavation many scholars remain cautious. Olmec engineering was ambitious: the earthworks and stone drains of San Lorenzo, the pyramid and courts of La Venta, and the transport of monuments weighing many tonnes without draught animals or wheels. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> balls of El Manatí show that people in the region were making rubber from the latex of the rubber tree, and perhaps playing the ballgame, by about 1600 BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote.jpg" alt="San José Mogote" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote">San José Mogote</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Jose_Mogote_Pyramid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maunus, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Objects and images in the Olmec style appear far beyond the Gulf Coast between about 1200 and 500 BCE: in burials at Tlatilco in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, on the reliefs of Chalcatzingo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morelos">Morelos</a>, in the painted caves of Juxtlahuaca and Oxtotitlán and at Teopantecuanitlan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote">San José Mogote</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-oaxaca">Valley of Oaxaca</a>, and on carved boulders at Pijijiapan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a>, Takalik Abaj in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and Chalchuapa in El Salvador.</p><p>The heartland had no jade or obsidian of its own, and its people obtained them through networks reaching across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>: obsidian from the highlands of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and Guatemala, jadeite from the Motagua valley, and iron ores for mirrors, some probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>. What they gave in return is less clear: perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, salt, shells or the finely made objects of their own workshops. Some monuments show figures who may be captives, and Olmec power may sometimes have rested on force, but there is little direct evidence of warfare.</p><p>How to explain this spread is one of the great debates of Mesoamerican archaeology. At a round table held at Tuxtla Gutiérrez in Chiapas in 1942, the Mexican archaeologist Alfonso Caso called the Olmecs the cultura madre, the “mother culture” of Mesoamerica, a view shared by Matthew Stirling and the artist and scholar Miguel Covarrubias. Others, notably Kent Flannery and Joyce Marcus, have argued that many regional societies, in Oaxaca, central Mexico and elsewhere, developed side by side and shared symbols through exchange, so that the Gulf Coast was one contributor among several. Chemical studies of pottery and new excavations have been used to support both views, and the discovery that the Maya centre of Ceibal had a formal ceremonial complex by about 1000 BCE, similar in plan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>’s, suggests that ideas moved in more than one direction. Most scholars accept that the Olmec centres were the largest and most elaborate of their time and that their art had wide influence, while the people of other regions adapted it to their own purposes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a> declined around 900 BCE, when many of its monuments were broken or defaced. Michael Coe and Richard Diehl, who excavated there in 1966–1968, at first saw this as the work of revolt or conquest; Diehl later concluded that monuments were often recarved and reused, and that the damage was part of that long practice. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a> flourished until about 400 BCE and was then all but abandoned, and the population of the eastern heartland fell sharply in the following decades. Archaeologists point to environmental changes that made the region less suited to large farming populations, such as rivers changing course and silting up, and perhaps eruptions in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuxtla-mountains">Tuxtla Mountains</a>, but the causes remain uncertain.</p><p>Olmec traditions did not vanish. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a>, on the western edge of the Tuxtlas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epi-olmec-culture">Epi-Olmec culture</a> developed an early writing system, and its Stela C bears a Long Count date of 32 BCE, one of the earliest known. The Olmecs’ images of rulers on thrones and stelae, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> god and the feathered serpent, and of sacred mountains and caves lived on in the art and religion of the Maya, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zapotecs">Zapotecs</a> and later peoples.</p><p>The rediscovery began in 1862, when a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal head</a> that had come to light at Tres Zapotes was described by José María Melgar y Serrano. In 1925 Frans Blom and Oliver La Farge of Tulane University described La Venta, and from 1938 to 1946 Matthew Stirling’s expeditions excavated Tres Zapotes, La Venta and San Lorenzo. Many scholars, among them the Maya specialist J. Eric S. Thompson, long thought the Olmecs later than the Classic Maya, until radiocarbon dates from La Venta in the 1950s confirmed their antiquity. In the 1950s many of La Venta’s monuments were moved to a park museum in Villahermosa, created by the poet Carlos Pellicer, as the oil industry spread over the site. Speculation, revived in the 1960s and 1970s, that the colossal heads portray Africans who crossed the Atlantic has no support among Mesoamerican scholars: the heads portray local rulers.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Olmecs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>’s Gulf Coast created the first great centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-venta">La Venta</a>, between about 1200 and 400 BCE. Known for their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a>, jade carving and buried offerings, they gave lasting form to Mesoamerican ideas of rulership and religion, and their art spread far across the region, though historians still debate whether they were its mother culture or one of several sisters.</em></p>

